   {"q": "Rank EV, Marshallian CS and CV for a price fall on a normal good, and say what drives the gaps.", "a": "`EV ≥ CS ≥ CV`. The gaps are entirely income effects. Under quasi-linear preferences the income effect is zero and all three coincide, which is the only case where ordinary consumer surplus is exact.", "difficulty": "advanced"},
   {"q": "Which measure is theoretically correct, WTP or WTA — and what decides it?", "a": "Property rights. If the person holds the right to the environmental good, removing it requires compensation, so WTA is correct. If they hold no right, they must buy the improvement, so WTP is correct. The choice is institutional, not technical.", "difficulty": "intermediate"},
   {"q": "Give four explanations for the empirical WTA–WTP divergence, strongest first.", "a": "(1) Hanemann's substitution effect — with no close substitutes the CV–EV wedge can be arbitrarily large; this is the strongest theoretical account. (2) Endowment effect / loss aversion from prospect theory. (3) WTP is bounded by income while WTA is not, producing protest and infinite bids. (4) The pure income effect — correct but far too small. Plus moral refusal to price environmental loss at all.", "difficulty": "advanced"}
