   {"q": "What exactly separates weak from strong sustainability?", "a": "The assumed elasticity of substitution between natural and manufactured capital. Weak treats them as substitutes and requires only total capital to be non-declining; strong holds that critical natural capital has no substitute and its loss is irreversible, so natural capital itself must be non-declining. The dispute is empirical, not philosophical.", "difficulty": "intermediate"},
   {"q": "State the Hartwick rule and give three criticisms.", "a": "Invest all scarcity rents from exhaustible resources into reproducible capital and constant consumption is sustainable forever. Criticisms: it needs high substitutability (capital share > resource share in Cobb–Douglas); it assumes continuing technical progress; rents are hard to measure and most resource economies show negative genuine savings; and it ignores the environment's sink function.", "difficulty": "advanced"},
